L1: Effect of carbohydrate–protein supplementation on endurance training adaptations
L2: European Journal of Applied Physiology (2020)
L3: Abdullah F. Alghannam, Iain Templeman, Joel E. Thomas, Dawid Jedrzejewski, Samuel Griffiths, Joseph Lemon, Thomas Byers, Sue Reeves, Javier T. Gonzalez, Dylan Thompson, James Bilzon, Kostas Tsintzas, James A. Betts
L4: 1 grid.449346.8 0000 0004 0501 7602 Lifestyle and Health Research Center, Health Sciences Research Center, Princess Nourah Bint Abdulrahman University,  Riyadh, 84428 Saudi Arabia
L5: 2 grid.7340.0 0000 0001 2162 1699 Department for Health,  University of Bath,  Bath, BA2 7AY UK
L6: 3 grid.35349.38 0000 0001 0468 7274 Department of Life Sciences,  University of Roehampton,  London, SW15 4JD UK
L7: 4 School of Life Sciences, University of Nottingham, Queen’s Medical Centre, Nottingham, NG7 2UH UK
L8: Purpose
L9: To examine the influence of post-exercise protein feeding upon the adaptive response to endurance exercise training.
L10: Methods
L11: In a randomised parallel group design, 25 healthy men and women completed 6 weeks of endurance exercise training by running on a treadmill for 30–60 min at 70–75% maximal oxygen uptake ( V O 2max ) 4 times/week. Participants ingested 1.6 g per kilogram of body mass (g kg BM −1 ) of carbohydrate (CHO) or an isocaloric carbohydrate–protein solution (CHO-P; 0.8 g carbohydrate kg BM −1  + 0.8 g protein kg BM −1 ) immediately and 1 h post-exercise. Expired gas, blood and muscle biopsy samples were taken at baseline and follow-up.
L12: Results
L13: Exercise training improved  V O 2max  in both groups ( p  ≤ 0.001), but this increment was not different between groups either in absolute terms or relative to body mass (0.2 ± 0.2 L min −1  and 3.0 ± 2 mL kg −1  min −1 , respectively). No change occurred in plasma albumin concentration from baseline to follow-up with CHO-P (4.18 ± 0.18 to 4.23 ± 0.17 g dL −1 ) or CHO (4.17 ± 0.17 to 4.12 ± 0.22 g dL −1 ; interaction:  p  > 0.05). Mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR) gene expression was up-regulated in CHO-P (+ 46%;  p  = 0.025) relative to CHO (+ 4%) following exercise training.
L14: Conclusion
L15: Post-exercise protein supplementation up-regulated the expression of mTOR in skeletal muscle over 6 weeks of endurance exercise training. However, the magnitude of improvement in  V O 2max  was similar between groups.
L16: Original Article
L17: Sucrose; Amino acids; Post-exercise nutrition; Recovery; Running
